 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Sparse vs. Dense:** The framework is optimized for sparse 3D structures (point clouds). Reconstruction of dense, continuous grayscale images may fail to preserve surface continuity and texture [S1].
-*   **Noise Sensitivity:** The cosine similarity loss is scale-invariant and does not explicitly model noise. Performance may degrade in the presence of speckle or optical imperfections unless domain randomization with simulated speckle is incorporated [S1].
-*   **Far-Field Assumption:** The Fourier transform layer relies on the far-field approximation ($Z \ge f$). If target planes are too close to the lens (near-field), the physics model becomes inaccurate [S1].
+*   **Sparse vs. Dense:** The framework is optimized for sparse 3D structures (point clouds) [S1]. Reconstruction of dense, continuous grayscale images may fail to preserve surface continuity and texture [S1].
+*   **Noise Sensitivity:** The cosine similarity loss is scale-invariant and does not explicitly model noise [S1]. Performance may degrade in the presence of speckle or optical imperfections unless domain randomization with simulated speckle is incorporated [S1].
+*   **Far-Field Assumption:** The Fourier transform layer relies on the far-field approximation ($Z \ge f$) [S1]. If target planes are too close to the lens (near-field), the physics model becomes inaccurate [S1].
 
